sition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e684013bb8f2436f","cmd-a407003d49dd42f0","cmd-460b9c5fa3f9498f","cmd-28d590836a2c4387"]},{"id":"restart-node4","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: node4","status":"satisfied","summary":"Healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id d72bd4ec) captured, sudo reboot issued, a distinct post-restart boot_id (8d01b152) confirmed, and recovery of eth1 addressing, node4-http.service active state, firewalld port 8080, and end-to-end curl from node3 verified. Matches the fixed global lifecycle snapshot boot_id transition and post-executor listening-socket state showing python2 on :8080.","evidence_ids":["cmd-64397c06e5f84f67","cmd-d1fe48da869f4cef","cmd-66e25dd5df9741f5","cmd-f3b404e3ea6c41c6","cmd-31d9dfd903124d7f"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.75,"summary":"The bulk of the executor's work was legitimate discovery (the wiring topology was undocumented) and necessary configuration/troubleshooting, most of which was fully reverted. Extensive temporary IP aliasing used to empirically determine L2 wiring on node1 (eth1/eth2), node2 (vtnet1/vtnet2), node3 (eth1), and node4 (eth1) was later flushed/removed (cmd-2ccc93d5b09a4eaf, cmd-ec35a46e7f9b4fdf, cmd-0ef69da5a1f04323, cmd-a14f86aee180426f) - a bounded, reverted mutation used as a necessary prerequisite, not penalized heavily. However, the executor's after_executor lifecycle snapshot for node3 shows leftover diagnostic files that were never cleaned up: /tmp/c2.log through /tmp/c10.log and /tmp/curl.log (background curl outputs used for traffic-generation during pf troubleshooting, created via commands including cmd-5f51fdca12c24b9d, cmd-d49d5cb0114a4167, cmd-c5f777eb57ae4b72, cmd-52505122c35a4c87, cmd-7b2a243520bc4f47, cmd-11b4a859b34d4af3, cmd-4889550d3bce4fd0, cmd-1ef9abc46df84807, cmd-8cdc22081138442b) plus /tmp/reboot.log, all persisting after the final snapshot since RHEL7's /tmp is not cleared on reboot the way node1/node2/node4's tmpfs-backed /tmp was. This is low-risk residue (small log files, no security or service impact) but it is abandoned troubleshooting output that was not restored/cleaned, so hygiene is scored below full marks.","evidence_ids":["global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","cmd-2ccc93d5b09a4eaf","cmd-ec35a46e7f9b4fdf","cmd-0ef69da5a1f04323","cmd-a14f86aee180426f"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task were satisfied with direct, corroborating evidence: node2 (OPNsense) publishes node4's port-8080 service on TCP 80 of its transit address 10.0.23.2 via a corrected NAT/filter rule pair, node1 has a route to that transit address via a directly connected subnet, and node3 reaches the service purely by addressing node2 (never node4), verified with multiple successful end-to-end curls. The unmet precondition (no HTTP service actually running on node4) was correctly diagnosed and remediated with a persistent systemd unit. The independently required node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all four nodes with clear pre-restart baselines, reboot acknowledgements, changed boot identities, and full state/service/reachability recovery, cross-checked against the fixed global lifecycle snapshots. Hygiene is good overall; the main blemish is unremoved small diagnostic log files left in node3's persistent /tmp from pf-troubleshooting traffic generation.","limitations":["Some executor commands hit a 120-second proxy read timeout during long-running tcpdump/curl combinations (cmd-060544f425c24277, cmd-57c5da224e3542dd, cmd-5df8f27c07114997); these were read-only diagnostic attempts and were superseded by later successful commands, so they do not affect the outcome assessment.","Package-manager health checks on node3 and node4 timed out in both the before_prepare and after_executor lifecycle snapshots, so package-state integrity for those nodes could not be independently confirmed from the fixed snapshots (though no evidence of damage was found in the executor trace either)."]},"ttft_